Question:
"We are working on a clone of Facebook. We want to add a numeric count to every post showing how many friends the post’s author has at the time of viewing the post, like this:
Our database has two tables:
 
USER
user_id (primary key)
name
created_date
 
USER_RELATIONSHIP
friendship_id (primary key, unique to each relationship)
user1_id (indexed)
user2_id (indexed)
start_date
 
Focusing on the database, how would you implement the friend-count feature?
Note we will soon be more popular than Facebook, so the solution needs to scale."



___________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________

"To implement this at scale, we have to look at the read-to-write ratio. A social feed is heavily read-heavy—posts are viewed millions of times a minute, but friendships are formed much less often.

If we use the naive approach and run a COUNT(*) query on the USER_RELATIONSHIP table every time a post is viewed, our database will instantly become a bottleneck. Counting rows is an O(N) operation, and doing that on every post load will crash the system.

Because of this, we need to shift the computation from the read path to the write path. My solution is database denormalization.

First, I would update the schema by adding a friend_count integer column directly to the USER table, defaulting to zero.

Next, we change the write path. When User A and User B become friends, we wrap the operation in a database transaction. We insert the new record into the USER_RELATIONSHIP table, and in that same transaction, we update the USER table to increment the friend_count by 1 for both users. We do the exact reverse for unfriending. To prevent race conditions if a user gets multiple requests at once, the update must be an atomic operation—like friend_count = friend_count + 1—rather than calculating the count in the application memory.

This completely optimizes the read path. When a user loads their feed, fetching the author's friend count is now just an O(1) lookup directly from the USER table.

Finally, to truly hit 'Facebook scale,' reading from a SQL database for every post is still too slow. I would put a distributed cache, like Redis, in front of the database. When the feed loads, the application fetches the friend count from RAM. When a new friendship is formed, we would use an asynchronous message queue, like Kafka, to update the SQL database and the Redis cache in the background so the user never experiences a delay."
